@begin(section)

@title(Quickstart)

This quickstart is meant to be illustrative. For ideas on how to use dsolv in
real life, have a look at @c(A Longer Example).

Given these artifacts:

@begin(list)
@item(@c(http://example.com/repo/a-1.0.zip))
@item(@c(http://example.com/repo/b-2.0.zip))
@item(@c(http://example.com/repo/b-3.0.zip))
@end(list)

@begin(enum)
@item(Generate dscard files to represent them in a dsolv repository, like
this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv generate-card \
    --id "a" \
    --version "1.0" \
    --location "https://example.com/repo/a-1.0.zip" \
    --requirement "b>2.0" \
    --card-file "$PWD/a-1.0.zip.dscard"

dsolv generate-card \
    --id "b" \
    --version "2.0" \
    --location "https://example.com/repo/b-2.0.zip" \
    --card-file "$PWD/b-2.0.zip.dscard"

dsolv generate-card \
    --id "b" \
    --version "3.0" \
    --location "https://example.com/repo/b-3.0.zip" \
    --card-file "$PWD/b-3.0.zip.dscard"
@end(code))

@item(Generate a @c(dsrepo) file from the cards:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv generate-repo-index \
    --search-directory $PWD \
    --index-file $PWD/index.dsrepo
@end(code))

@item(Then use the @c(dsrepo) file to resolve dependencies. Resolving the
package @c(a) returns its full dependency closure:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv resolve-locations \
    --repository $PWD/index.dsrepo \
    --requirement "a"
@end(code)

This should return something like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
b==3.0 @ https://example.com/repo/b-3.0.zip
a==1.0 @ https://example.com/repo/a-1.0.zip
@end(code))
@end(enum)

To see the help page, call @c(dsolv) or any of its subcommands with the
@c(help) subcommand, or the @c(-h)/@c(--help) alias. If this is your first
time using dsolv, it's recommended that you read @c(A Longer Example).

@end(section)
